<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hellespontine Phrygia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lesser Phrygia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hellespontine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, or Lesser Phrygia, was a Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> (province) in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, directly southeast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>, with its capital at Dascylium. Its first Achaemenid ruler was Mitrobates, appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>, and from about 479 BCE the satrap Artabazus and his heirs, the hereditary Pharnacid dynasty, ruled it for most of its existence. In 334 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> gave it to his general Calas, who held the Persian title of satrap and collected the tribute once paid to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a>. It then passed to Leonnatus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-corupedium">Battle of Corupedium</a> in 281 BCE and was finally integrated into the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their wealth came from estates across the empire, from the king’s gifts and from office: Darius’s uncle Parnaka ran the economy of the homeland around Persepolis in 506–497 BCE, and his son Artabazus founded a line of satraps in <b>Hellespontine Phrygia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li></ul></section>
